    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent start, it's pretty accurate. But it's a bit too brief for a study guide. Can you expand on each of those phases (Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase)? \n\nTry to go into more detail about what's actually happening with the chromosomes and the cell membrane in each stage so the students can really grasp the mechanics. Also, we really need to make sure this hits our quality threshold for the ranking tool, so just make sure it feels pedagogically sound.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To better help students understand mitosis, we can break down each stage with more detail. During Prophase, the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, and the nuclear envelope begins to break down. In Metaphase, these condensed chromosomes line up perfectly along the cell's center, known as the metaphase plate. Anaphase is when the sister chromatids are pulled apart by spindle fibers toward opposite poles of the cell. Finally, Telophase reverses the early steps, reforming the nuclear envelopes around the two new sets of chromosomes as the cell divides.",
      "source": "target",
